# markdown-for-llms: the one file of settings and keys of the product in containers.
#
#   1.  cp .env.example .env        the file stays on this machine: git ignores it
#   2.  edit .env                   every line below is a default: change only what you need; put a key only
#                                   for a remote service you want to use
#   3.  make up                     checks this file, builds the images, prepares the toolchain image and the
#                                   weights, starts the services and prints the address
#
# After a change: `make restart` (the containers keep the file they were started with; an editor that saves by
# replacing the file leaves them the old one, and the routes with a key stop until the restart).
#
# Who reads what. `make` and compose read only MDF_PORT, MDF_UPLOAD_MIB and the MDF_COMPOSE_* names. The `config`
# step reads the MDF_ lines, validates them and gives them to the services; unknown MDF names and invalid values
# stop `make up` with the reason, and so do settings the archive would refuse (see "Archive"). The keys at the
# end are read by name, by the worker and the `mdf` tool only, each time one is used; they never enter the
# environment of a container. `make up` gives this file mode 0600; a link, or a file of another user, is refused.
# docs/how-to/run-in-containers.md

# --- Where the product keeps things on this machine (read by `make` and compose only)
# The archive (your documents and their conversions) and the state (the spend ledger of the rented GPUs,
# servers.json, the cache and the runs of the bench): folders of yours, by default in this checkout. A relative
# path is relative to the checkout.
# MDF_COMPOSE_DATA=./data
# MDF_COMPOSE_STATE=./state
# How many children of the engine (tool and Marker containers) may run at the same time.
# MDF_COMPOSE_MAX_CHILDREN=8
# Memory caps of the services themselves (the children have the cap of MDF_MEMORY_MIB each).
# MDF_COMPOSE_API_MEMORY=1g
# MDF_COMPOSE_WORKER_MEMORY=4g
# MDF_COMPOSE_CLI_MEMORY=4g

# --- Archive and processes
# The address of the Web UI and of the API: 127.0.0.1 only, on this port (a port of the block of the project).
MDF_PORT=23465
MDF_HOST=127.0.0.1
# The settings from here to "Conversion engine", with MDF_THREADS, MDF_MEMORY_MIB, MDF_PROCESS_LIMIT,
# MDF_MAX_PAGES, MDF_OUTPUT_MIB, MDF_LOG_MIB, MDF_MAX_PIXELS and MDF_MAX_DIMENSION below, are part of the IDENTITY
# of an archive: once an archive exists they cannot change (`make up` and `make restart` stop and name what
# changed). Put the value back, or start a new archive in another folder (MDF_COMPOSE_DATA). Only
# MDF_STORAGE_MIB can change, with the product stopped: `make down`, `mdf configure --expected-config ID
# --storage-mib N` (ID is the value of "config" in identity.json of the archive), the same N here, `make up`
# (docs/how-to/run-in-containers.md, "Settings that cannot change"). MDF_PORT is not part of the identity.
# The only profile: conversions run on the engine, which records its versions in engine.json.
MDF_BIND_PROFILE=engine-v1
MDF_REVIEW_PROVIDER=disabled
# Archive quota, without automatic deletion; free reserve space kept distinct.
MDF_STORAGE_MIB=20480
MDF_RESERVE_MIB=1024
MDF_UPLOAD_MIB=50
MDF_POLL_SECONDS=1
MDF_LEASE_SECONDS=15
MDF_GRACE_SECONDS=5
# Fixed in the containers, where compose decides the paths: leave them commented (a value here stops `make up`).
# The archive is moved with MDF_COMPOSE_DATA.
# MDF_DATA_ROOT=/data

# --- Conversion engine (the worker and mdf convert)
# The 18 weight files of the Marker route (indra-ocr v1.1.0 and GoNoto) are in the models volume, prepared by
# `make up` (`make prepare`) and verified before every conversion; nothing is downloaded during a conversion.
# Fixed in the containers: leave it commented. Another folder is refused with `invalid_setting`: the children
# read the weights from that volume alone.
# MDF_MODEL_ROOT=/models/marker
# Total deadline of a conversion in seconds; 0 = none (default): stop it with Cancel.
MDF_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=0
# Stop a conversion whose child writes no progress event and uses almost no CPU time for this long (minimum
# 60). 900 is an initial default, not a guarantee.
MDF_STALL_SECONDS=900
# Memory cap, without swap, of each child of the engine (a tool container, the Marker container).
MDF_MEMORY_MIB=16384
MDF_THREADS=4
MDF_PROCESS_LIMIT=512
# The most pages of one document. It is also the floor of a GPU session of the archive: a session opens only with a
# reserve that holds an attempt of MDF_MAX_PAGES pages, so a larger value raises its minimum reserve.
MDF_MAX_PAGES=50
MDF_OUTPUT_MIB=512
MDF_LOG_MIB=10
MDF_MAX_PIXELS=25000000
MDF_MAX_DIMENSION=16000

# --- Datalab, a remote route chosen per document
# Spending cap in USD of one conversion with Datalab; 0 (default) sends nothing. With a cap above 0 and
# DATALAB_API_KEY below, the Web UI offers Datalab with the transit notice and a mandatory confirmation: the
# document leaves this machine only then. `make status` says what is missing. Not part of the archive identity.
MDF_DATALAB_CAP_USD=0

# --- GPUs of the marker-server route in the archive; none of these is part of the archive identity.
# The GPU presets this instance enables: comma-separated names of markdown_for_llms/gpu/data/presets.json, only
# supported presets on a network volume: the rented modal-base and runpod-serverless-volume-dc, and the GPUs of the
# user gpu-local (this computer, `make gpu-up`) and gpu-remote (`make server-up` on another machine), designed and
# tested with fake servers only; empty (default): none.
# MDF_GPU_PRESETS=
# Caps in USD of the ledger for each provider (0, the default: the presets of that provider are not available),
# and of one GPU session (its reserve A: at least the minimum reserve of the preset with MDF_MAX_PAGES pages). Only
# the rented presets read them: gpu-local and gpu-remote cost nothing and have no session.
# MDF_GPU_CAP_USD_MODAL=0
# MDF_GPU_CAP_USD_RUNPOD=0
# MDF_GPU_SESSION_CAP_USD=0
# Fixed in the containers: leave them commented. The endpoints of the instance, without secrets, are the file
# config/servers.json of the state folder (MDF_COMPOSE_STATE; an example is next to this file,
# servers.example.json); the spend ledger is its folder gpu-ledger.
# MDF_SERVERS=/state/config/servers.json
# MDF_GPU_LEDGER_DIR=/state/gpu-ledger

# --- The GPUs of the user (read by `make` and compose only; docs/how-to/run-in-containers.md). An NVIDIA card of
# 24 GB, a driver with CUDA 13 or newer, the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, amd64. Memory cap of the container of vLLM:
# MDF_COMPOSE_GPU_MEMORY=16g
# On the machine of the remote server only (`make server-up`, compose.server.yaml; never on the machine of the
# product): its address on the LAN or the VPS, an IPv6 one between brackets ([2001:db8::10]); never the loopback
# nor every address, in IPv4 or in IPv6 (127.0.0.1, 0.0.0.0, [::1], [::]): the product reaches it from another
# machine; its port (a port of the block of the project), the folder of its TLS certificate (server.crt,
# server.key) and the file of its bearer key, both private files of yours (mode 0600). A relative path is relative
# to the checkout.
# MDF_COMPOSE_SERVER_ADDRESS=
# MDF_COMPOSE_SERVER_PORT=23467
# MDF_COMPOSE_SERVER_TLS=./state/server/tls
# MDF_COMPOSE_SERVER_KEY=./state/server/key

# --- mdf bench (`make bench`): its cache and its runs are in the state folder, under bench/. These names are read
# by `mdf bench` alone, and compose sets the two folders in the environment of the `bench` and `cli` containers:
# leave them commented here. Of a settings file `mdf bench` reads MDF_BENCH_CACHE_DIR only by the commands that
# take a settings file (prepare, list, fetch, checks); `run`, `report` and `crosscheck` read the environment or
# their options (--cache, --runs-dir). Ports of the project block.
# MDF_BENCH_CACHE_DIR=/state/bench/cache
# MDF_BENCH_RUNS_DIR=/state/bench/runs
# MDF_BENCH_RELAY_PORT=23461
# MDF_BENCH_INFERENCE_PORT=23463
# Memory cap of each external tool call of the bench (reference toolchain container), MiB, 64-1048576.
# MDF_BENCH_TOOL_MEMORY_MB=8192
# Default of `mdf bench run --spend-cap-usd`: the cap in USD (0-1000) of the Datalab spend ledger of the runs
# folder (spend.jsonl), all runs together.
# MDF_BENCH_CAP_DATALAB_USD=4.00

# --- Keys of the remote services: here and nowhere else, never in git.
# One NAME=value per line. The worker and the `mdf` tool read a key by name from this file each time they use it;
# no key is ever in the environment of a container, in a log, in the archive or in the output of a command
# (`mdf bench secret-scan` checks it). The names of the keys of an endpoint (key_env of servers.json) end in
# _KEY, _SECRET, _TOKEN, _TOKEN_ID or _BEARER. Names only here (docs/how-to/convert-documents.md):
# Datalab, the remote route chosen per document:
# DATALAB_API_KEY=
# RunPod, an API key restricted to the endpoint of the marker-server route (Authorization: Bearer):
# RUNPOD_API_KEY=
# Modal, the proxy token of the workspace of the app (Modal-Key and Modal-Secret, `wk-...` and `ws-...`); not
# the token of the SDK, which is the next one:
# MODAL_PROXY_TOKEN_ID=
# MODAL_PROXY_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Modal, the token of the SDK (`ak-...` and `as-...`, from the settings of the workspace on modal.com): the worker
# gives it to each process of the Modal CLI in a private file of its own; no ~/.modal.toml is read in the containers:
# MODAL_TOKEN_ID=
# MODAL_TOKEN_SECRET=
# The same proxy token as one bearer, <token id>.<token secret>, for an endpoint of kind `user` on Modal:
# MODAL_PROXY_BEARER=
# Your own GPU server (`make server-up` on another machine; the preset gpu-remote): its bearer key, the content of
# the key file of that server (MDF_COMPOSE_SERVER_KEY there). Designed and tested with fake servers only:
# GPU_SERVER_KEY=
